To resolve Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) errors in Next.js when using WordPress as the backend, you can follow these steps:

### 1. Configure CORS on WordPress

WordPress does not natively support CORS, but you can configure it to allow requests from your Next.js app.

#### Step 1: Add `allow` Header to Your API Endpoints

In your WordPress REST API endpoints, add an `allow` header to specify which domains are allowed to access them. For example:

```php
// functions.php or similar file
function custom_rest_allow_headers() {
    return array(
        'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' => '*',
        'Access-Control-Allow-Methods' => 'GET, POST, OPTIONS',
        'Access-Control-Allow-Headers' => 'Content-Type, Authorization'
    );
}
add_filter('rest_cors_headers', 'custom_rest_allow_headers');
```

This will allow all origins to access your API endpoints.

